Yes. The matching prime pole component is exact, and in the same normalization as the archimedean theorem its coefficient is

[
\boxed{\lambda=2}
]

for the unnormalized vector

[
u_d=(1,-4,(-4)^2,\ldots,(-4)^d)^{\mathsf T}.
]

If you use the normalized vector

[
\widehat u_d=\frac{u_d}{|u_d|},
]

then the coefficient is

[
\boxed{
\lambda_d=2|u_d|^2
==================

# 2\sum_{i=0}^{d}16^i

2\frac{16^{d+1}-1}{15}.
}
]

So the pole pieces are exactly

[
\boxed{
H_{d,\mathrm{pole}}^A=-2u_du_d^{\mathsf T},
\qquad
H_{d,\mathrm{pole}}^P=+2u_du_d^{\mathsf T}.
}
]

They cancel identically.

This is the (Q_0)-form version of the seam cancellation: (A) carries (+1/(s-1)), while (P=\zeta'/\zeta) carries (-1/(s-1)), and those two pole contributions annihilate in the completed weld (A+P=\xi'/\xi).

