## Theorem — prime pole rank-one component

Let

[
P(s)=\frac{\zeta'}{\zeta}(s),
\qquad
w=s-\frac12.
]

Near (s=1), write

[
\zeta(s)=\frac{\eta(s)}{s-1},
\qquad
\eta(1)\neq0.
]

Then

[
P(s)
====

# \frac{\zeta'}{\zeta}(s)

-\frac1{s-1}
+
\frac{\eta'}{\eta}(s).
]

So the prime-side singular part is

[
P_{\mathrm{pole}}(s)=-\frac1{s-1}.
]

In seam coordinate,

[
s-1=w-\frac12,
]

so

[
P_{\mathrm{pole}}!\left(\frac12+w\right)
========================================

-\frac1{w-\frac12}.
]

Now define the prime-side seam coefficients by

[
S_k^P
=====

-\frac12[w^{2k-1}]P!\left(\frac12+w\right),
]

and

[
T_k^P=(-1)^kS_k^P.
]

The pole part expands as

[
-\frac1{w-\frac12}
==================

# \frac{2}{1-2w}

# 2\sum_{n\ge0}(2w)^n

\sum_{n\ge0}2^{n+1}w^n.
]

Thus the coefficient of (w^{2k-1}) is

[
2^{2k}.
]

Therefore

[
S_{k,\mathrm{pole}}^P
=====================

# -\frac12\cdot 2^{2k}

# -2^{2k-1}

-\frac12,4^k.
]

Multiplying by ((-1)^k),

[
T_{k,\mathrm{pole}}^P
=====================

# (-1)^kS_{k,\mathrm{pole}}^P

-\frac12(-4)^k.
]

Now the (Q_0) Hankel block has entries

[
H_d^P[i,j]=T_{i+j+1}^P.
]

So the pole contribution is

[
H_{d,\mathrm{pole}}^P[i,j]
==========================

-\frac12(-4)^{i+j+1}.
]

But

[
-\frac12(-4)^{i+j+1}
====================

2(-4)^i(-4)^j.
]

Hence, with

[
u_i=(-4)^i,
]

we get

[
\boxed{
H_{d,\mathrm{pole}}^P
=====================

2u_du_d^{\mathsf T}.
}
]

