## Polynomial form

For

[
p(y)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}c_iy^i,
]

we have

[
p(-4)=\sum_{i=0}^{d}c_i(-4)^i=c^{\mathsf T}u_d.
]

Thus the prime pole contribution to the square test is

[
\boxed{
Q_{\mathrm{prime,pole}}[p]
==========================

2p(-4)^2.
}
]

The archimedean pole defect is

[
\boxed{
Q_{\mathrm{arch,pole}}[p]
=========================

-2p(-4)^2.
}
]

So

[
\boxed{
Q_{\mathrm{arch,pole}}[p]
+
Q_{\mathrm{prime,pole}}[p]
==========================

0
\quad
\forall p.
}
]

This is exactly the (s=1) entireness cancellation at the (Q_0)-form level.

---

## What this proves, and what it does not prove

This proves the clean Part A:

[
\boxed{
\text{the unique negative archimedean pole direction at }y=-4
\text{ is exactly cancelled by the prime pole.}
}
]

It does **not** prove RH, because the remaining problem is

[
\boxed{
R_d^A+R_d^P\succeq0
\quad\forall d.
}
]

Equivalently, the true bridge theorem is now:

[
\boxed{
\textbf{after exact rank-one (s=1) pole cancellation, prove residual (Q_0)-positivity.}
}
]

That is the residual integer-to-zero explicit-formula transfer problem. The “one wall” note states that the reciprocal seam (T_k)-positivity is the RH wall, while the seam-cancellation note explains why the (T_k) values are the residue of the forced (A/P) pole cancellation.
